Bob Ceculski wrote:
> forget emulators ... you will kill roughly half of the cpu ... i had a
> brief stint on an ibm as400 running system34 emulation under os400 and
> it absolutely killed the cpu ... hp unix doing the same thing on itanic
> will perform worse!


But look at how 68000 emulation runs on MAC with powerpc chips. If you are
running the same OS with the same system services (which was not the case with
AS400 emulating another OS). 

Where emulation does wonder is when you can recompile perhaps 90% of your
applications, including the core stuff and only the last 10% is "lost" and you
can't find the source code etc. Having the ability to emulate that less
important 10% gives you the ability to move everything to the new platform and
drop the old one.

Compare this to messaging on VMS where Digital chose not to port Message
Router, forcing those who migrated to Alpha to keep VMS nodes to run the MTA
backbone.  Having built-in emulator would have allowed shops to also move the
MR stuff to their new alpha machine.  (In hindsight, since Digital was intent
on giving that market where it used to be a leader to Microsoft, it is
understandable that they chose to break its legs and let it fall on its own).


