Alternately, render the shared user account not-login-able.

Edit /etc/passwd, the <shared_user> line, replace the encrypted password+salt
(between 1st & 2nd ':')
with something the encryption algorithm is _extremely_ unlikely to generate. 
E.g., '*', or '*NoLogin*'.

shared_user:*:uid:gid:GCOS comment field:homedir:shell

Specify /bin/rsh for the shell if you are feeling generous, /bin/true if nasty.

Above to be modified appropriately in the presence/use of shadow password file
and/or enhanced security.
Should prevent 'su - shared_user' as well.

=Aaron


