In article <b9cd0ee3.0110240547.66692e9e@posting.google.com>, erevos@mailbox.gr (Erevos) writes:

>Hello,
>I am using a XP1000 workstation with True64 unix v4.0f
>
>I would like to ask if there is any way to find out if the hard disc has bad blocks.

By reading every sector of it.  In addition to this, some storage protocols
may support reading the disk in a way that doesn't require sending the data
over the interconnect.

Someone else suggested scu(8), which is fine for SCSI connected disks.
Be careful to understand the difference in the verify and scan
command of scu(8).  One simply reads a range of sectors.  The other
writes the sector first and then checks the data written.  I'm pretty
sure verify is the one that is read-only, but I always check first.

For disks connected to a backplane array controller (KZPSC, KZPAC, etc)
see what the configuration utility has.  If it doesn't have anything
you can use dd(1) with a suitable transfer size.
