As an old but unrepentent RSX and VMS user, I have to observe that the
absolute fastest way to stream large files into memory is to do so
explicitly, using asynchronous multi-buffering that bypasses any system
caching (and the overhead of per-line system calls).  Not only does this
eliminate the overhead of copying data from system cache to application
memory (assuming the system doesn't do something suave in the way of simply
remapping it for at least read-only access), but it avoids depending upon
whatever system algorithms may be doing in the way of intelligent pre-fetch
(and those algorithms are almost sure to be some kind of compromise rather
than speed-optimal).

Depending upon how a given system handles mapped files compared with
explicitly-read files, mmapping may or may not be as fast:  when one issues
an explicit read the system at least knows how much data is needed, whereas
using mmap it just knows you need the next page (and may or may not retain
access-history information to give it a clue about how much more than a
single page to ask for from disk in case you'll soon want to touch more).
In either case, you're still at the mercy of whatever the system designer
thought was best (if s/he thought at all) rather than in direct control of
your destiny.

- bill

