It is "possible" that the 8600 might have had that code, but the 780 is
highly unlikely.  The work happened around the time the 9000 was being done,
and sadly the engineer doing the work has since passed away.  The problem
with the code that does it, was that it took a lot of time to try and pin
down memory usage such that we could "guess" that it was "only" a process
page, and kill the process - of course, the process might be something
*really* important, and you never know when some oddball thing has a PFN
cached away someplace.

On Alpha, in any case, _any_ double bit error will crash the system.  Single
bit errors are ECC corrected, and logged.  On a future Alpha platform,
memory RAID will be an option, which will allow multi-bit error correction.


