I've spent a little time before trying to make reads of large files as
fast as possible.  I'm sorry that I don't recall exactly what the
hardware was, I think the os was 4.0D, and the c++ compiler was around
6.1.  The short of it was that std::iostream's were fairly slow.
Using std::getline into a std::string was very slow for large lines
(big lines, like 100k).  I didn't see much difference between kernel
read() and mmap(), but both were *much* faster than std::iostream.

I was a little disapointed that iostream was this slow.  I expect a
10% penelty for using iostream, but it was worse than that.  While
read()/mmap() was much faster, it still seemed a little slow to me.
Seems like something like 6meg/second, but I realize that this number
doesn't help at all without knowning what the hardware was.  I was
also a little surprised that read() and mmap() had very similar
performance.  I guess it shouldn't be too much of a surprise with a
very fast (and wide) memory bus.  (I tried mmap() all at once, or in
pieces, using various madvise() calls, and all had the same
performance.)  

Again, this has been about a year ago, so could be old hardware, old
os, and my failing memory.  I to would be very interested to hear from
others who found a faster way to read large files.

Later,
Rob


