Fred Kleinsorge wrote:

>  And yes, we have people buying Alpha today, and porting to Alpha
> today - knowing that down the road Itanium will be the platform.

It will be _A_ platform, but it's really bad planning to assume that it will be
_THE_ platform, except
for maybe a year or two. The Q is getting out of the CPU business, so the VMS
engineers should at least
consider the advantages of finally making the OS effectively platform
independent..


You know, the reason that Intel can foist the Itanium on us is because it held
a near monopoly
on desktop (NOT server) chips for 15 years.   It seems to have escaped
everyone's attention that
other than the x86 series none of Intel's other CPUs have been a success.  That
is, they could
leverage the monopoly, but never broke away from it.  Currently the Itanium
costs roughly the same amount
as an Alpha (and the few systems I've seen start at a lot more than a DS10).
Which is a long way
of getting back to pointing out that the AMD Hammer line has a much better
economic strategy
than does the Itanium.  Assuming for the sake of argument that both chips
actually work more or
less as advertised and both companies stay afloat then we have:

Hammer  Itanium
Y              Y            fast 64 bit
Y              N            fast 32 bit /  runs old Windows 32 bit code well
Y              Y            runs new Windows XP (or whatever) 64 bit well
Y              N            viable desktop chip

Which means that at the end of 2002 Joe Consumer can upgrade his P3 desktop to
a Hammer and keep his software,
but he can't upgrade to an Itanium and do the same thing.  So the Hammer
potentially has a  lower unit
cost than does the Itanium because it will sell in volume.  That doesn't mean
that Intel won't be selling a lot of P4s, but
that does nothing for the unit price of the Itanium.   So servers built with
Hammer's will potentially
cost less than those built with Itaniums.  And they'll run more software - all
the existing x86 code, apparently - it will
be years before the Itaniums specific versions of most software appears. Of
course, there is the possibility that Intel can keep HP and Dell completely
AMD free (so much for the "free" market),  but that won't stop ASUS and the
others from building Hammer motherboards, and it won't stop any of us from
going to any local computer store and ordering one.   That's how I buy my PCs
now in any case - it lets
me pick exactly the components I want.  Something like 30% of the desktop
market comes through similar channels now,
and if in 2 years even half of that is running Hammer, and the rest of the
desktop market is P4s, then the Hammer
production run will be vastly larger than the Itanium's.

For that matter, even Apple will likely sells 10X more Power chips than Intel
will Itaniums, and with OS 10 they can
start leveraging that into the small server market and might expand their
market share.

Alas the powers that be have long since decreed that VMS shall only run on huge
iron, which is really a pity because clusters of cheap
computers (Hammer's, PowerPC, whatever - CHEAP is the important thing)running
VMS would have been attractive for a lot of current applications, and very
attractive for future ones, such as distributed databases.  Instead there will
only be huge costly SMP machines - high margins, low market penetration - and
most people will never again set eyes on a VMS box.

Regards,

David Mathog
mathog@caltech.edu



