May 14, 2000


Michael Capellas
Chief Executive Officer
Compaq Computer Corporation

cc:  Richard Marcello


Dear Mr. Capellas:


We are an international group of computer industry professionals with
diverse backgrounds who have recently banded together to discuss concerns
about Compaq's relative neglect of its OpenVMS operating system.  Our hope
is that a coherent presentation at the highest level may be more effective
in communicating the adverse effects of such neglect than the constant but
muted customer rumbling that has occurred over the years.

We appreciate your time and attention and will try to be concise.  In turn,
we hope you will be tolerant of the level of occasionally-blunt detail we
deem necessary to break through long-standing barriers to changes in the way
OpenVMS has been handled.  It appears that some such changes may be taking
place even as we speak:  please do not mistake our attempt to present a
complete picture for any lack of appreciation for the efforts already under
way.

Our own discussions have ranged more widely and deeply than can reasonably
be presented here.  Should you find the following material useful and have
interest in further contact, we would be happy to talk with you or other
appropriate parties.


Sincerely yours,

<names excised to protect the still-shy>

plus several contributors who have elected to remain anonymous



Part I:  Why OpenVMS?  Why Now?


OpenVMS's Role Within Compaq

There may be a natural tendency to concentrate on the intense direct
competition that the rest of Compaq's products face with their external
look-alikes and an understandable reluctance to risk disrupting OpenVMS's
significant and at least relatively uncontested revenue stream.  If so, the
view inherited from Digital that OpenVMS is a 'legacy' system nearing its
twilight years likely reinforces any inclination to leave well enough alone.
However, such treatment ignores the role OpenVMS could play in
differentiating Compaq from vendors such as Dell, Sun, and HP, whose
offerings consist primarily of commodity Windows and Unix products, and in
placing Compaq on equal footing with IBM, which currently does a far better
job of leveraging the full breadth of its systems.

OS/360-370-390 is a respected 36-year-old architecture that remains a
leader, despite running on limiting 32-bit hardware, and IBM has taken full
advantage of it over the years as it explored additional markets with newer
products.  OpenVMS was once a similarly respected system but is now largely
ignored, despite running on arguably the fastest and most scalable 64-bit
commercial hardware in the world, because Digital decided long ago that
'non-proprietary' systems were its future and did its best to convert its
existing customer base to their use.

Since it remains impossible to forecast when, if ever, Unix (let alone
Windows) will be able to adequately replace OpenVMS (or OS/390), IBM's
strategy of integrating its proprietary crown jewel seems to have proven
superior to Digital's strategy of replacement.  Unfortunately, corporate
decisions of such a fundamental nature carry with them a great deal of
inertia, not least because admitting failure can compromise high-level
careers, and that inherited inertia apparently continues to influence the
attitude of Compaq toward OpenVMS to this day.


But OpenVMS is so Proprietary

So are OS/390 and OS/400 - perhaps more so, if one includes hardware
multi-sourcing considerations.  And while customers and third-party vendors
do gravitate toward the familiarity and increased vendor-independence
offered by Unix or Windows solutions when such solutions satisfy their needs
cost-effectively, when this is not the case they still readily turn to
full-service vendors for 'proprietary' solutions as long as they have
confidence that the vendor, and the solution, will continue to meet their
requirements over time.

Despite over a decade of marketing neglect, OpenVMS has maintained its
technological leadership and remains superbly qualified for this role.  It
is significantly superior to any commonly-used Unix (let alone Windows) in
the areas of reliability, availability, scalability, disaster-tolerance,
security, near-real-time activities (e.g., process control), I/O-intensive
workloads, record-oriented processing, and mixed-language applications.
Other notable strengths include ease of administration, support for
application development, and performance/operating efficiency.

Given the degree to which expectations have been lowered over the years by
Windows and Unix and their associated hype, we feel a need to emphasize that
these distinctions are real.  OpenVMS can credibly claim to be the most
scalable system on the planet:  it is suitable for the smallest start-up
operation, yet can never be outgrown.  It has levels of security found in no
other commercial operating system, clustering technology that remains years
ahead of any competition, and a degree of fault-tolerance and resistance to
error that allows OpenVMS to be used in mission-critical systems that must
stay up for years at a time (the longest we know of is 17 years without a
reboot in the service of the Irish National Railway).


OpenVMS as The Compaq Differentiator

Sole ownership of OpenVMS gives Compaq the ability to react quickly to new
customer needs and attract business by offering features that Unix and
Windows environments cannot match (see above).  Customers dependent upon
unique OpenVMS features become tied to Compaq to a degree that
'non-proprietary' customers are not - a healthy relationship as long as
Compaq views it as symbiotic rather than exploitative.  Combine this lock-in
with the common desire, especially among large customers, to deal with a
single vendor, and other vendors who lack such soup-to-nuts range in their
product sets become second-class contenders in a significant percentage of
sales situations.

IBM has long used its OS/360-370-390 architecture to anchor the high end in
such situations and the niche solutions supported by its OS/400 systems to
anchor its presence in other environments.  If Compaq can demonstrate
effectively that it now views OpenVMS as a core asset rather than as an
aberration in its product line-up, OpenVMS can once again become the kind of
keystone that clinches corporate-wide standardization on a single vendor and
stabilizes, rather than frustrates, customer loyalty.

Ideally, all of Compaq's systems should be able to win sales on their own
merits.  However, the intense competition for advantage in Intel
Architecture hardware platforms (including IBM's OnForever robust hardware
and X Architecture heterogeneous clustering initiatives), the continuing
apathy toward Tru64 in the Unix market, and the partnerships Dell has formed
to sell servers and server-appliances and to counter its weakness in service
and support are only a few of the factors conspiring to make it difficult
for Compaq to hold the Windows and Unix ground it already occupies, let
alone gain any.  Linux may change the nature of the Unix playing field
somewhat, but here again competitive pressures are strong, with IBM
committing to support Linux environments on all its systems - including
OS/390 and OS/400 - as well as positioning S/390 systems as general-purpose
servers (an important AlphaServer market segment).

A highly-visible renewed commitment to OpenVMS is one of the few ways in
which Compaq can differentiate itself to gain entry into and/or advantage in
corporate sales situations for all its products.  It is also an opportunity
to break the long-standing stranglehold that industry doubts about OpenVMS's
future have held on one of Compaq's highest-margin revenue streams.

The sections below outline some of the current obstacles to such
revitalization and suggested remedies for them.  Since we recognize that
Compaq has a great deal of experience in how to market products that it
wants to market, most of this discussion is presented in abbreviated form,
which we would be happy to expand upon if desired.



Part II:  Problems/Remedies


Advertising

Unless Compaq puts its money where its mouth is, no one is going to believe
that a renewed commitment to OpenVMS is any more real than previous
temporary blips on the otherwise blank radar screen.  How much money?
Compare the net profit generated by PC and Industry-Standard Server sales
and service to that generated by OpenVMS sales and service.  Now compare
their respective ad budgets, in all media (not including AlphaServer ads:
they almost never mention OpenVMS save as an afterthought to a Tru64 pitch).

While that provides a first approximation of the degree of change required
for credibility, OpenVMS may not need nearly as much general advertising as
long as it has industry advertising comparable to Windows'.  For a start, it
needs something like two-page "OpenVMS Advantage" ads in the same
publications (and with the same frequency) as the "Windows 2000 Advantage"
ads so that not only the OpenVMS name but detailed information about its
capabilities will reach the people who approve corporate purchases, as well
as those who recommend them.  (We understand that the Windows 2000 Advantage
ads are shared with Microsoft, but many of Compaq's other Wintel platform
ads are not.)

OpenVMS advertising needs to emphasize the OpenVMS strengths (see earlier
list) that other systems, including Compaq's, lack, just as advertising for
other Compaq systems emphasizes their own perceived strengths (such as the
recent UK ad headlined "Nothing integrates better with NT than TRU-64
UNIX" - ahem).  This is difficult to do tactfully when an ad is shared with
other Compaq systems, though ancillary, less specific shared advertising can
be effective too as long as the systems are featured equally.  OpenVMS also
needs to be advertised to the rest of Compaq's current customers in the same
manner other Compaq systems are advertised to the OpenVMS base.

Additional specialized OpenVMS advertising is warranted in markets deemed
especially receptive to OpenVMS - certainly the current 'target market' set
plus any environment (e.g., Web services of all kinds) requiring a
highly-available, highly-scalable, and/or highly-secure platform.  However,
it is important not to perpetuate the impression that those markets are the
only areas in which OpenVMS is actively competing:  a system perceived to be
holed up in a few remaining strongholds is not one corporations will choose
to commit to for the next decade or more.  Less concentrated but visible
specialized advertising in any market segment where OpenVMS can effectively
compete will provide important reassurance of Compaq's dedication to a
strategy of broad-based, long-term growth.

All the above won't begin to use up the amount of money suggested above as
necessary for credibility, but it will at least be a good start.  It likely
has more directly-measurable benefits than generic 'brand awareness' print
and television advertising (though the competition does not shrink from
funding this).  And it should provide initial evidence suggesting whether
further increases would prove profitable.


Web Information

Once people are made aware of OpenVMS, it is imperative that they be able to
find out virtually anything they may want to about it easily, including
information about purchasing and pricing, available applications, and
up-to-date benchmark results (a critical factor in some sales).  While we
recognize that Compaq has been working hard to improve OpenVMS information
accessibility via the corporate Web site, we have yet to see anything to
suggest that the desired result will appear in the foreseeable future.

There are too many examples of Web-site problems even just to list here,
which leads us to suspect that the underlying issue may be structural -
perhaps a result of over-centralization of content-control rather than
leaving it in the hands of the people best equipped to present it
effectively (under suitable overall corporate look, feel, and taste
guidelines), un-muddled by frequent inter-mixing of references to Windows
and Tru64.  Whatever the cause, prompt action is important, and once again
we would be happy to offer detailed suggestions if asked to.


Corporate Publications and Presentations

While the decidedly inadequate OpenVMS Web presence may merely be the result
of corporate ineptitude, the consistent disregard for OpenVMS in Compaq
publications and presentations that glowingly showcase Windows, Tandem NSK,
and Tru64 presents the disturbing appearance of an active conspiracy of
silence.  When

Compaq's Annual Report fails even to mention OpenVMS in its corporate
narrative despite an 'unstoppable' theme that OpenVMS fits better than
anything else save NSK,

the January Investor Relations presentation by Enrico Pesatori is similarly
silent,

the Inform corporate newsletter presents a 'success story' without noting
that the platform used was OpenVMS,

the Compaq Non-Stop eBusiness Strategy white paper treats OpenVMS more like
SCO Unix and NetWare than like Compaq's other main-line systems,

the set of AlphaServer Web links is overwhelmingly Tru64-related,

it conveys a definite message to both existing and potential customers that
OpenVMS is not a priority for Compaq.  No competitor could instill the
degree of 'Fear, Uncertainty, and Doubt' about OpenVMS that Compaq's own
actions (and Digital's before it) have:  pervasive, long-term change will be
necessary to erase it.


Sales

Similar FUD regarding OpenVMS has been generated by the persistent efforts
of the sales force to encourage happy OpenVMS customers to migrate to some
other Compaq platform.  We hope that recent rumors of change in this area
are true:  not only does it tend to lose Compaq entire corporate accounts
(if they migrate, it is often to a competitor), but it's really annoying.

We also have anecdotal evidence that when OpenVMS is being considered for a
new installation, along with some other Compaq system, failure to pursue the
OpenVMS option often results in loss of the entire sale to some other vendor
(e.g., OpenVMS was competitive due to its unique qualities whereas Tru64 was
not).


Press Relations

Compaq does a commendable job of releasing information about its PC and
Industry-Standard Server offerings and a more modest but adequate job of
releasing information about AlphaServer hardware and Tru64 Unix.  But
(again) there is only silence where OpenVMS is concerned, and as a result
media coverage routinely categorizes OpenVMS as 'aging', 'legacy', or
'defunct' (all have appeared recently).  A bit of targeted personal
schmoozing might also help:  the return could be substantial.


Application Availability

The fact that customers often choose an application and only then choose a
hardware platform means that system sales can often be dependent on the
range of available applications - an area in which OpenVMS has become
seriously deficient.  Visible renewed commitment by Compaq to OpenVMS sales
across the full market spectrum will start to encourage Independent Software
Vendors and Value-Added Resellers to make applications available on OpenVMS.
Even minor additional effort specifically aimed at courting and educating
ISVs and VARs could achieve disproportionate results.  For especially
strategic applications, consider providing hardware, consulting, or even
financial aid.

Examples of enterprise applications not available in their current versions
on OpenVMS include SAP, Oracle Applications, Baan, Peoplesoft, Sybase,
Informix, DB2, MS SQLserver, Netscape Server Suite, Lotus Notes, MS
Exchange, MS IIS, Squid, MS Office, Corel Office, Lotus Smartsuite, and Star
Office.  The much shorter list of such applications that are available -
Oracle, Oracle RDB, PMDF, and Apache - does not compare favorably with
competing platforms.


New Product Features

Published road maps for OpenVMS development do not present a picture of a
system committed to continued leadership of the kind it enjoys today.  This
makes knowledgeable customers hesitant to commit their own operations to it
for the long term.  Targeted new development can also be a real bargain
compared with other promotional mechanisms, and we offer two examples for
consideration:

The currently-defunct OpenVMS POSIX environment originally gave rise to the
'Open' in OpenVMS.  We have heard that efforts are under way to resurrect it
in a more usable form, with better performance and one hopes improved
integration with the native environment (i.e., much more than just a 'check
the box' facility).  Extension to allow it to run many/most Alpha Linux
binaries on OpenVMS would not only help make a large library (1200+) of
sorely-needed applications instantly available but would match IBM's 'Linux
everywhere' strategy (see
http://www10.nytimes.com/library/tech/00/03/biztech/articles/20soft.html and
http://www.redherring.com/insider/2000/0503/tech-ibmlinux050300.html -
interesting reading in multiple respects).  An efficient and integrated
Linux persona coupled with the existing strengths of OpenVMS would create a
platform unequaled in the industry.

A second initiative which would mesh nicely with such Linux support is
development of a heterogeneous SAN file system to allow concurrent
data-sharing across OpenVMS, Windows 2000, and Unix/Linux.  Though IBM, HP,
Veritas, EMC, Adic, Avid, and likely others have products in this space, it
remains an area where no one yet appears willing to make the commitment to
do the job right.  This represents a leadership opportunity that Compaq,
with its expertise across OpenVMS, Tru64, and NT environments, is unusually
well-positioned to avail itself of.  It also offers improved integration of
heterogeneous systems within single Wildfire boxes, dovetails well with
Compaq's strengths in lower-level storage architecture, and protects against
industry standardization on some competitive implementation that does not
include OpenVMS and Tru64.


OpenVMS in Academia

We applaud rumors of a recent decision to offer free OpenVMS licenses to
academia.  In combination with existing free academic cluster licensing, it
should help stimulate use of OpenVMS in areas long ago lost to free Sun,
*BSD, and Linux configurations.

While we cannot prove it, we suspect that the decline of OpenVMS in the
marketplace is at least in some small part attributable to its disappearance
from academia (where it once reigned supreme) and its resulting
unfamiliarity to the current generation of purchasers, users, and support
personnel.  If so, additional efforts in this area (e.g., strategic
applications and video and/or on-line tutorials that could be used as course
adjuncts) could prove fruitful.


Entry-Level System Pricing

Aggressive pursuit of entry-level sales (like attention to academia and
serious on-going development) tends to reassure customers about a vendor's
long-term commitment to a system, since there is little immediate, direct
payoff for the effort expended.  One of our members stated it thus:

"Without seeds, you don't get seedlings.  Without seedlings, you don't get
new trees.  A huge, elegant, mature tree surrounded by asphalt and concrete
and maintained lawn and pretty little flowerbeds in a city square is a dying
tree.  In contrast, a patch of fertile land covered in nondescript scrub can
be a forest of such trees in the making.

"I think people may appreciate this at an instinctive level.  No OpenVMS in
colleges, no small OpenVMS sites, no affordable low-end OpenVMS, means a
dying operating system, and the only question is how long the remaining
trees will stand for."

We will not presume to suggest what Compaq's trade-off between low-end
profit and market penetration should be, but offer the following
observations for consideration:

The low-end market is especially sensitive to the relationship between
hardware and system software price.  This suggests making at least the
system software price platform-specific rather than shared across, say,
DS10s, DS20s, and ES40s (or a new sub-DS10 platform should one appear).
Pre-packaged 'cluster in a box' configurations and volume discounts starting
at quantity 2 for multi-box sales might also stimulate interest.

The price of an entry-level OpenVMS system will inevitably be compared with
Windows 2000 Server or Windows 2000 Advanced Server (the latter including
primitive clustering support).  Given the superior capabilities of OpenVMS
and the fact that entry-level DS10 hardware costs about twice as much as
good-quality Intel hardware, one can justify pricing a DS10 OpenVMS software
configuration at about twice the level of a similar Windows software
configuration.

Price it much higher than that level, and even OpenVMS enthusiasts will
start to question its value; price it noticeably lower, and the market will
perceive it as aggressive.  In this context, note that Windows 2000 Server
and Advanced Server include features such as TCP/IP and other networking
support, disk defragmentation, and volume shadowing that are extra-cost
options in OpenVMS and should thus be added before comparing pricing.
Furthermore, Windows system prices are typically discounted when bundled
with a hardware sale.



Conclusion

Because OpenVMS has suffered years of neglect compared with other Digital
and competing systems, temporary compensatory efforts may be necessary to
let the world know in no uncertain terms that OpenVMS is not only still
alive but hunting for bear.  We otherwise suggest that all Compaq platforms
will have the best chance of realizing their potential if they attempt to
exploit their mutual synergy (a strategy that IBM embraces whole-heartedly
within its own product set) rather than partition themselves into boxes that
at least some customers will not find to their liking.  In the process,
Compaq will return to a business model that served Digital well at the
height of its success - and be seen as more responsive to its customers as
well.





