Look at what happened between Windows 3.1 and Windows 95/NT. People switched
because of social pressures, marketing, but also because they wanted to get
out of that indecent DOS/Windows combination.

Windows 98/2000/ME/XP/whatever were just incremental changes. No pressure to
do a corporate-wide upgrade for desktops.

However, imagine the scenario where Windows-** on IA64 servers provides all
sorts of nifty LAN management features which won't be available on the 32 bit
version. That will provide some incentives for corporations to move their
servers to 64 bits, and eventually their desktops when prices make IA64
commodity (if it ever happens).


The big question in my mind is the sort of incestual relationship that exists
between Microsoft and Intel. If Intel has a deal with Microsoft, then you can
be assured that Microsoft will take whatever decisions needed to make IA64 the
de-facto standard and kill off Hammer.


