In the Tru64 v5.1A Release Notes manual ARH8WDTE
Section 4: Processor Specific Notes reads as follows:
\begin{quotation}
        4.3    AlphaServer GS Systems
        The following notes are specific to AlphaServer GS systems.
        . . . <text removed> . . .
        4.3.2    Do Not Repetitively Power Cycle CPUs
        The use of OLAR management commands in continuous test loops 
        can degrade the reliability of the CPU. These commands remove the
        DC power source from the CPU module. We recommend that you do not 
        power cycle CPUs repetitively with shell scripts.

        The CPU module's DC to DC converter is specified to have a maximum 
        of 1000 power cycles. Do not exceed his number of power cycles for a CPU.
        . . . <text removed> . . .

        4.4    Personal Workstation 433au, 500au, and 600au Systems
        The following notes are specific to Personal Workstation class systems.
\end{quotation}

My reading leads me to conclude that some component on
an Alpha GS system has a MTBF of around 1000 days if I were to 
flick the switch on each morning and off each evening.  

Hence on average I could expect the hardware to last 1000 days before
requiring service or replacement given the usage pattern described.  Perhaps
such systems are designed assuming they will not be power cycled every day?

Reading on; no such statistics are quoted in Section 4.4 for PWS systems.
Nor were they quoted in Section 4.2 for Alpha Server 1000 systems.

What sorts of design assumptions were made for the PWS/AS1000 systems
regarding power-on/power-off usage patterns?

How many times can they be power cycled before a failure?

Do they have the same cpu modue DC-DC converter as the GS's ?

Are there any Alpha hardware folks (preferably design/reliability engineers,
etc) or others "in the know" that can comment on these matters?

Thanks



