E Haywiser <ejh1@mit.edu> wrote in message news:<9q2v39$9dn$1@bob.news.rcn.net>...
> Reading on; no such statistics are quoted in Section 4.4 for PWS systems.
> Nor were they quoted in Section 4.2 for Alpha Server 1000 systems.
> 
> What sorts of design assumptions were made for the PWS/AS1000 systems
> regarding power-on/power-off usage patterns?
> 

The power cycling section has to do with running a command to perform
online replacement of CPU cards in the GS series.  The GS series run
on 48 VDC power and I'm sure that circuitry on board is just to remove
power to safely remove the card without hosing the rest of the QBB. 
For that design, 1000 power cycles for 1000 removal/insertions of one
particular card could be considered overdesign.

There is no correlation between a million dollar piece of hardware
designed to run for year(s) at a stretch and the older technology
workstation/workgroup class machines you mention.  There is no online
replacement for those platforms since they are CPUs directly mounted
to the motherboard.

As far as daily powerup/downs on a workstation or workgroup class
AS1000, I don't see why that would be a problem since their power is
nothing fancier than your run of the mill Intel server.  Some defense
contractor probably powercycles their Alpha CAD workstations in the
name of security every shift.  I don't see why a department class
4100/ES40/ES45 would have an issue with daily powercycling for that
matter.

Keep in mind that powercycling any PC/Server puts stress on the
components.  Those stresses are incorporated into the design of the
box, but still, powering up and down any component over the long term
is going to wear it out quicker than not doing so(i.e. hard disks,
fans, and anything eslse mechanical).

John


