Please don't put words in my mouth.  I expect that had we continued to
develop the EV8 that it would have remained "competetive" with Itanium in
performance, but I doubt it cost.  The cost to do it apparently did not
justify the investment, and it only would have gotten worse over time.

EV6 was a pretty good leap in technology, and it was hard to do - busted a
lot of software along the way to get it right.  The OoO stuff, speculation,
using cache state for multiprocessor locking, etc.  EV7 doesn't really
change anything there, instead it focuses on things that are really outside
of the processor - like the cost of getting to memory, and how to glue
together a NUMA system without a costly hierarchical switch.  EV8's focus
was mostly on how to leverage the resources on a single die which are
normally inefficiently used - which doesn't really help with single stream
performance.  The processor speed, and number of units on the ship would
have increased that somewhat - and made it "competetive" with Itanuim.

But how much faster does it have to be to "win" in the market place?
Clearly there have been times when we have had well over a 2x performance
lead - but that didn't translate into it eating SUNs lunch - who have a
pathetic turd-on-a-chip called Sparc.  Yes, it made us the standard for
people who were willing to pay *any* price for the highest performance - so
the genome people love it, some animation shops love it, some of the
military love it, and some of the supercomputer people love it. The VMS
people love it BECAUSE IT RUNS VMS.

We have a lot of experience building relatively large servers out of Alpha.
EV7 will provide really fast systems from 2 processors up to 64
(architecturally 256, and realistically 128 could be built).  EV7, and EV79
will remain competetive with everything - including the Power4.  But Itanium
*will* catch up within a performance delta that is immaterial to all but
zealots, and eventually surpass it.  We can all take wild guesses at what
that timeframe is.  And we can all speculate about when and how fast a EV8
and EV9 would have been.

But I contend that most people, aside from the zealots, do not really care
about the processor ISA.  They care about the price/performance - and that
only a handfull of people are way out on the leading edge of wanting to pay
for absolute performance.  What they do care about is competetive pricing
with competetive performance in the industry.  And that their software
investment is protected.

We are porting to Itanium.  We will do our best to make it as painless a
transition as possible.  People who need raw performance, and need it before
say 2004/2005, can safely buy EV6x and EV7x based systems with the
appropriate price/performance.  When Itanium comes along, it will at first
be for early adopters - and won't be as fast as existing Alpha platforms.
But it WILL be able to run Windows as well as VMS, something we can no
longer say about Alpha.  People can make apples-to-apples comparisons
between vendors offerings - since pretty much everyone except SUN will be
selling Itanium systems.  And at some point - and I am not smart enough (or
paid enough) to know *exactly* when, Itanium family systems will be as big,
bigger, fast, and faster than Alpha platforms.

I think with enough money, Alpha could have been performance competetive
with Intel and IBM.  But it would have taken a lot of money to stay out in
front of both, and the delta between performance might not have been large
enough for someone to pick Alpha over Itanium - especially in the UNIX
space.  I think not having control over the fab makes it tough to compete on
raw performance.



