Please. please. please.  Do not respond to me.  I made a mistake and
responded to your assertions.  You want to parse things into nice neat
little bundles.  I'm a liar or an idiot, or both.  Not a  __ing thing will
alter that in your universe.  This isn't a court of law, or a Columbo
episode - and the truth (and opinion) is seldom self consistant.  You want
to postulate performance characteristics, time to market and cost - all
fabricated out of pretty much hearsay - good *and* bad.  Spin on.  Enjoy
yourself.

I'll stick to these loose assertions:  Alpha was great.  Loved it.  Had a
great time.  A decision I had no part in making was that it was not cost
effective to invest in the Alpha ISA long term (at this point I really don't
care if the reason is valid or not, or if *you* agre with it), and to switch
to the Itanium architecture - for good or ill.  People making a lot more
money than I do believe that Itanium will be competetive, and we have EV6x
and EV7x until it is.  I myself will hedge on the exact date.   The
performance of the mythical EV8 or EV9 are not interesting, as they will not
be built.  We will be on an industry standard architecture, offered by
everyone except (perhaps) SUN.  "Hammer" will never be embraced by
Microsoft, and will quietly die.  IA32 will be phased out by the end of the
first decade of the 2000's, as both Intel and Microsoft "churn" the user
base to force PCs to be replaced.  Linux will follow (or lead).  Most of our
customers will be happy with the VMS to Itanium transition, which will give
them compatetively priced hardware.  A minority will make a decision to move
onto another vendors OS.  We will do everything in our power to make the
transition long, and smooth.

It was foolish of me to A) say anything about Itanuim, and worse B) respond
to anything you write.

I promise.  promise.  promise to not respond to you, and to ignore you from
now on.  It was my mistake.

Really.  I have figured out how to work the newsgroup filters, I just had
been avoiding it in this group.

_Fred

