YOU WOULD THINK THAT now IBM has an answer to the Q Wildfire platform, it
would reduce rather than raise prices, wouldn't you?
No. In the new-style Compaq, the rule is to put the prices up - and while
they're at it, charge well over the odds for memory too. (Hi Kingston.)
The Q put prices of its GS 1001MHz CPU by $1,000 for the GS80 and the
GS160/320. These, no doubt, are the coppa whoppas IBM is making for them and
it's hard to grasp exactly why the price has risen so much.
However, the 731MHz CPU price stays the same. Q's justification for the
price hike is that it is "insignificant at a system level".
If it, sorry HP, is moving everything to INTC chips, it had better learn
that the CPU price is far from insignificant in Xeon and Itanic servers....
Q has actually lessened the price of its memory for DS boards by nearly 40
per cent, while the 1GB memory has dropped by a quarter. The reduction for
DS20E systems are around a fifth too.
This makes the memory cheaper but still astonishingly expensive given the
state of the DRAM industry.
But, we suspect, this is also "insignificant at a system level". �




