I have run into an NFS problem that I believe has something to do with the
way Tru64 UNIX version 4.0F or newer NFS clients work. The NFS server is
running OpenBSD version 2.9. I have set up an export that currently has no
restrictions other than mapping root to another account.

I am able to mount the share using NFS V3 from a Digital UNIX 4.0B client
without a problem.

However, an attempt to mount the share from a Tru64 UNIX 4.0F or higher (5.1
included) client results in the following error message:

# mount -t nfs /home@xxx.xxx.xxx.xxx /foo
NFS3 RFS3_FSINFO failed for server xxx.xxx.xxx.xxx : RPC: Authentication
error
/home@xxx.xxx.xxx.xxx on /foo: I/O error

Any idea on what is happening here? I have tried using NFS V2, but that does
not help other than generating a different error message.

# mount -t nfs -o nfsv2 /home@216.144.26.157 /ftp
NFS2 getattr failed for server xxx.xxx.xxx.xxx : RPC: Authentication error
/home@xxx.xxx.xxx.xxx on /foo: I/O error

I have tried both UDP and TCP just in case that works - nope! The mountd on
the NFS server reports (mountd is running in debug mode) that the mount was
successful even though the client does not mount the share. I have read that
the Linux NFS server code has an option that allows the server to disable
locking, enabling Tru64 UNIX clients. I wonder if the issue exists with *BSD
NFS servers also. If it is relevant rpc.lockd is running on the NFS server.

TIA,

George.






