>restrictions other than mapping root to another account.

>I am able to mount the share using NFS V3 from a Digital UNIX 4.0B client
>without a problem.

Not much changed between V4.0B and V4.0F other than a number of performance
changes.

>However, an attempt to mount the share from a Tru64 UNIX 4.0F or higher (5.1
>included) client results in the following error message:

># mount -t nfs /home@xxx.xxx.xxx.xxx /foo
>NFS3 RFS3_FSINFO failed for server xxx.xxx.xxx.xxx : RPC: Authentication
>error

Well, the client must have made it through the mount protocol.  The FSINFO
call is one of to NFS calls done during mount.  I'm not familiar with OpenBSD,
but I'd think that if the mount protocol returns a file handle, the
client ought to be able to access it via NFS.

You say you're mapping root to another account.  I wonder if there's anything
there.  Try mapping to uid 65534 (nobody) and uid 0 (if your network is
secure enough)
> I have read that
>the Linux NFS server code has an option that allows the server to disable
>locking, enabling Tru64 UNIX clients. I wonder if the issue exists with *BSD
>NFS servers also. If it is relevant rpc.lockd is running on the NFS server.

and rpc.statd?  Shouldn't matter, files aren't locked during mounting.

Sorry I don't see what's really happening.  I'd concentrate on the server,
I don't know what group you should have crossposted to other than
comp.protocols.nfs.

