I did some more testing this morning. The OpenBSD server is on the DMZ port
of a firewall, while the Tru64 UNIX clients are all on a secure LAN behind
the firewall. The LAN port is NATed and hence all traffic from the Tru64
UNIX clients appear to be from a single IP address, but different
non-reserved ports.

If I place the OpenBSD server on the LAN in the same subnet (no NAT) as the
Tru64 UNIX clients, the mounts work without a hitch on the 4.0F and higher
clients. So it appears that NAT is causing the problem between the server
and >= 4.0F clients.

On the OpenBSD server there is an option to allow NFS mounts from
non-reserved ports, and that is enabled allowing the 4.0B client to mount
without a problem through the firewall with NAT enabled.

George.

