In article <3BC3E02A.51F4D013@videotron.ca>,
JF Mezei  <jfmezei.spamnot@videotron.ca> wrote:
> Also, Digital bet on NT
> way too early in the game. And Compaq knows that NT isn't quite ready yet, but
> my guess is that as soon as they see NT mature enough, they will continue what
> Digital had begun. Until then, everything is on the backburner, including VMS.

Do you have any reason to believe that NT will ever be a credible enterprise
class operating system? The fundamental design of the OS and API is
relentlessly single-instance: having more than one privilege, application,
or session instance on an NT server causes problems that do not seem to
be addressible, even using the new "session" concepts in Terminal Server.

A UNIX or VMS box can host multiple protection domains, multiple versions
of applications, and multiple independant sessions of a single user instance,
without difficulty. They have been able to do this for over two decades. You
can't even run two versions of IIS concurrently on an NT server without
using some desperate workaround like VMWARE.

These restrictions are buried deeply in the Win32 subsystem, and in some cases
in the NT kernel itself. I don't see how they can be fixed without fundamental
and incompatible design changes in the OS and all applications.

What solution to things like the single registry namespace do you see?

