Brian Haley wrote:

> Shawn wrote:
>
> > Just curious if anyone has had similar problems.  In one of our data
> > centers, some of our alpha systems lose network connectivity.  What we
> > end up doing is just a simple "rcinet restart" and then the system
> > continues working.
>
> Losing network connectivity is sort-of a vague description of a
> problem, can you answer a few questions?.  Are all connections
> hung or is it just that new ones can't be created (maybe inetd)?
> Can you reach some systems but not others (routing problem)?
> Do you have a lot of host routes on your system with the destination
> and gateway the same (icmp redirect problem)?
> Do you have permanent ARP entries that are getting modified
> (ARP problem)?
>

One other cause of losing network connectivity is the router.  We had a
problem where connectivity loss occurred because the router kept changing
speeds and duplex mode during the auto-negotiate phase..  If the router has
the ability to change line speeds, have your network techs change it to a
fixed speed.  Then, set the speed of your network interface using ifconfig.
There's nothing you can do about the duplex mode, but the router will still
auto-negotiate a correct duplex mode if the speed is fixed.

