Fully agreed. When I was involved relatively directly with balancing
budgets, I recall having to go through quite complex cost justifications
when extended warranties, plus uplift to the required service level, to
balance against the capital versus maintenance budgets. It also made a
nightmare for the depreciation calculations. They were never steep
enough as it was. In some cases kit had to be kept in storage until it
could be depreciated to a write off or other low enough value. Strange
but true.

It would probably be a good idea if they could offer extended warranty
as an add on, so you could budget which ever way was easiest. Quite a
lot of consumer electronics in the UK come with this type of option,
usually insurance backed. However I expect if Compaq were to offer 
warranty in this way, that they should have two separate part numbers,
because when a financial admin sees things like warranty uplift as a
separate item, they start shuffling budgets for you. 

As to this case in particular, we would always have to pay uplift to
whatever warranty to get the service level required for the equipment,
so nothing every really came free. Holds today. Unless the warranty has
a 2 hour response on it, it's not worth anything. A fair amount of kit
we've installed over the past 2-3 years is probably still "under
warranty" but we've paid maintenance from day 1.
